now in this video we will study the  trigeminal nerve or the cranial nerve v  this is the largest cranial nerve coming  to the introduction the trigeminal nerve  it arises from four nuclei three of  these nuclei are the sensory nuclei and  one is the motor nucleus now these four  nuclei combine and give rise to three  branches the v1 or the ophthalmic branch  the v2 or the maxillary branch and the  v3 are the mandibular branch of the  ophthalmic and the maxillary are purely  sensory and the mandibular branch is  mixed having both sensory and motor  component in the next section of this  video we will study how the trigeminal  nerve originates and next part of the  video we will study the course and the  distribution of these branches  now coming to the origin of the  trigeminal nerve I have drawn two views  here first is the lateral view and  second is the transverse section of the  pons the first coming to the lateral  view the this is the midbrain this is  the pons this is the medulla and this is  this spinal cord the upper part of the  spinal cord  now the trigeminal nerve arises I have  just mentioned before from four nuclei  the three of them are sensory and one as  the motor nucleus now the first nucleus  we are going to talk about is the  missing cephalic nucleus this is  situated in the midbrain and thus known  as a missing cephalic and the second  nucleus second sensory nucleus is the  principal sensory nucleus which is  situated in the bonds and the third is  this final nucleus which is a very long  nucleus and situated in the lower part  of the medulla and the upper spinal cord  now the one motor nucleus which is  situated in the pons and this is located  here now these three nuclei the missing  cephalic the primary sensory nucleus and  the spinal nucleus are the effin nuclei  this is the trigeminal ganglion  so these receive the afferent nerve  fibers from the trigeminal ganglion  these red fibers these green fibers and  the blue fiber which I have depicted  these are the F and nuclear different  fibers and this is the motor nucleus and  this is an efferent nucleus and this  sends these orange fibers which have  shown to the trigeminal ganglion which  run through the third division of the  trigeminal nerve  mandibular nerve and supply the muscles  of the first prinkle arch now this is  the transverse section of the pons  showing the origin of the trigeminal  nerve now this these three are the  nuclei of the trigeminal now at the  level of the pons now this is the  principal sensory nucleus this is the  missing cephalic nucleus in the middle  and this orange is the motor nucleus  spinal nucleus I have not shown in this  section because it is situated in this  section of the lower medulla and the  upper part of the spinal cord now let's  look at the course of the trigeminal  nerve its division into its main three  branches and then the subsequent  distribution on the face as I have  mentioned earlier the pons is the site  of the origin of the trigeminal nerve  this is the main trunk and this is the  trigeminal Canyon now the trigeminal  ganglion is situated in the middle  cranial fossa now in the temporal bone  there is a depression known as the  trigeminal cave which is the site for  the location of trigeminal ganglion from  this trigeminal ganglion the three main  branches the ophthalmic the maxillary  and the mandibular branch of the  trigeminal nerve arrives first let's  come to the first division that is the  ophthalmic division of the trigeminal  nerve now the ophthalmic division arises  in the middle cranial fossa from the  trigeminal ganglion it runs forward and  through the superior orbital fissure it  exits the cranium and divides into three  main branches now before exiting through  the superior orbital fissure the  ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal  now runs through the lateral wall of the  cavernous sinus three main branches  after exiting the superior orbital  fissure are the frontal branch the  necessary branch and the lacrimal branch  now coming to the area of distribution  of the autonomic branch of the  trigeminal nerve the area of  distribution is shown in this green part  and the frontal branch of the ophthalmic  division supplies the forehead the area  of the scalp and also it supplies the  frontal and the ethmoid sinuses the  nasal ciliary branch it supplies the  upper eyelid the cornea and the dorsum  of the nose  whereas the lacrimal branch which  receives the fibers from the Teleco  palatine ganglion it supplies the  lacrimal gland the second division of  the trigeminal nerve which arises from  the trigeminal ganglion is the maxillary  division now this branch also runs  forward and exits the cranium through  the foramen rotundum  now after exiting through the foramen  rotundum the maxillary brush divides  into fourteen branches and supplies the  middle part of the face now this branch  also runs through the latter part of the  cavernous sinus area of distribution of  the maxillary branches this area which  is shown in the pink and after its exit  through the foramen rotundum  the maxillary division divides into 14  branches which supply the lower eyelid  the conjunctiva the skin of the cheek  the maxillary sinus the nasal cavity  upper lip the molar incisors and the  canine upper canines and the superior  part of the palate a third division of  the trigeminal now the v3 is the  mandibular branch now this is the only  of the three branches which have  both motor and the sensory component  mandibular branch area is shown in this  yellow area after its exit to the  foramen ovale the mandibular branch  divides into four terminal branches the  buccal branch inferior alveolar or acute  temporal and the lingual branch and the  area of distribution includes the mucous  membrane of the oral cavity the external  ear lower lip the skin of the chin and  anterior 2/3 of the tongue the  mandibular branch also has the motor  component so it supplies the muscles of  the first branchial arch which includes  the muscle of the mastication including  the temporalis muscle the lateral and  the medial pterygoid the masseter muscle  it also supplies the tensor tympani in  the air and the tensor veli palatini of  the palate so this was an overview  regarding the trigeminal nerve I hope  you find this video informative please  support our efforts to make more videos  by subscribing to this channel and also  please leave a thumbs up for this video  thank you
 

